Scientific Claim–Evidence Traceability under Controlled Provenance Transformation

A Factorial Protocol for Causal Identification, Component Necessity, and Equivalence Testing

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract. 

Observational evidence at scale establishes that citation fidelity degrades along chains of scholarly transmission and that the degradation is conditioned on source accessibility, recency, and author-level characteristics. What observational data cannot establish is the causal decomposition: whether the absence of provenance metadata—persistent identifiers, paragraph locators, verbatim excerpts, claim–passage relations, and scope qualifiers—independently impairs the capacity of an evaluator to reconstruct the evidential basis of a scientific claim when the semantic content of that claim is held approximately constant. This proposal specifies a factorial protocol in which provenance level and semantic transformation are orthogonally manipulated under fixed access conditions, and in which the preservation of meaning is operationalized as a vector constraint rather than a binary entailment gate. The dependent variable is a stratified sequence of reconstruction outcomes—source identity, locator recovery, support judgment, and scope retention—measured for human evaluators and for a fixed-retrieval language-model agent analyzed through an error-structural rather than an accuracy-matching lens. The framework pre-registers equivalence testing alongside superiority testing, calibration-gated non-inferiority analysis for minimal sufficient records, and a hard constraint on the conditions under which an exponential decay parameter may be estimated. The scientific value of the programme resides not in the demonstration of degradation but in the causal separation of provenance absence from semantic drift, in the identification of component necessity, and in the production of a validated measurement architecture whose most consequential outputs may be threshold effects, interactions, or equivalences rather than a decay function.

1. The Identified Gap: Observational Precedent and Causal Separation

The phenomenon of provenance-associated verifiability degradation is not in question. An analysis of approximately 13 million citation sentence pairs establishes a “telephone effect”: when citing papers exhibit low fidelity to the original claim, downstream papers that cite both the intermediate and the original exhibit lower fidelity to the original . Citation fidelity is systematically higher when cited papers are more recent, intellectually closer, more accessible, and authored by teams with lower first-author h-index and medium size . This large-scale measurement renders redundant any proposal whose primary empirical claim is that traceability decays along transmission chains.

What the telephone-effect study and adjacent observational work cannot deliver is causal decomposition. A low-fidelity citation in naturally occurring scholarly text may arise because the citing author lacked a paragraph locator, because the citing author semantically compressed the claim, because the cited source was itself already drifted, or because the citing author’s access environment did not include a required supplement. These mechanisms are entangled in observational data. The quasi-experimental design of the telephone-effect study manipulates exposure to low-fidelity predecessors, not the availability of provenance metadata while holding the semantic content of the claim fixed.

The residual space is therefore a four-way conjunction: provenance is experimentally manipulated; semantic content is constrained at a meaning-preserving level; the dependent variable includes paragraph-level reconstruction rather than source identification alone; and the criterion is independent human reconstruction rather than an automated proxy. No existing benchmark or study satisfies all four conditions simultaneously. SciFact provides expert-written claims paired with evidence-containing abstracts and rationale annotations, and it defines the task of selecting evidence for or against a claim ; it does not ablate provenance. CiteME establishes that source identification from a passage is a measurable human task, with frontier language models achieving only 4.2–18.5% accuracy while humans achieve 69.7% at a mean of 38.2 seconds ; it fixes the stimulus-to-source relation and does not manipulate metadata richness. Citation-integrity annotation distinguishes accurate, inaccurate, and irrelevant citations in biomedical publications ; it classifies citations as they occur rather than manipulating the conditions under which they are evaluated. Evidence Inference 2.0 shows that 63.0% of evidence spans supporting clinical judgments lie outside the abstract and that 13.6% span more than one sentence ; it establishes the multi-locator structure of scientific evidence but does not test how the removal of locational provenance affects recovery.

The contribution of this programme is therefore the experimental separation of provenance-metadata absence from semantic transformation as independent causal inputs to reconstruction failure, under a meaning-preserving constraint whose satisfiability is itself measured and reported.

2. Construct Architecture: Six Separable Layers

A single “verifiability” score compresses distinct failure mechanisms. The framework separates six constructs, each with an independent measurement operation.

Source identity recovery is the identification of the correct publication, dataset, report, or other source artefact. Locator recovery is the location of the relevant sentence, paragraph, table, figure, supplement, or set of passages within or across sources. Evidence adequacy is the judgment of whether recovered material supports, contradicts, partially supports, or fails to determine the claim. Scope and limitation retention is the preservation of qualifiers, population restrictions, assumptions, uncertainty, and boundary conditions. Semantic fidelity is the retention by a transformed claim of the original proposition, including entities, quantities, relations, modality, and scope. Factual status is whether the claim is supported by the source-verified record, independently of whether any particular evaluator can recover that record.

These layers are not interchangeable. A source may be correctly identified while the selected passage does not support the claim. A claim may be true but difficult to verify under available provenance. A summary may remain semantically similar while deleting the limitation that renders the scientific interpretation valid. The layered architecture therefore replaces a single tuple of claim, source, and passage with a claim–evidence graph permitting multiple sufficient sources, non-contiguous passages, tables, figures, supplementary material, and inferential chains. The graph is represented in a PROV-compatible vocabulary of entities, activities, agents, derivations, and transformation events , with the dissertation adding a human verification layer to that established representation.

3. The Meaning-Preserving Condition as a Vector Constraint

3.1 The insufficiency of bidirectional entailment

A paraphrase that passes a bidirectional entailment check need not preserve the referential granularity required for paragraph-level localization. Adversarial paraphrasing research demonstrates that technical-term preservation and bidirectional entailment are independently varying constraints: an iterative evolutionary attack generator can produce paraphrases that preserve both entailment and technical terms at high rates, whereas synonym-substitution methods preserve entailment in only 38–61% of cases even when they pass a semantic checker . A paraphrase may preserve logical consequence while substituting a term whose referential granularity differs—for example, “p53 activation” for “p53 pathway activation”—and such substitutions change the paragraph at which the claim can be located without changing the proposition in the narrow entailment sense.

3.2 Preservation vector

Let a claim c be represented by the tuple

S(c) = \langle E(c),\, T(c),\, Q(c),\, R(c) \rangle,

where E(c) \in \{0,1\} is bidirectional entailment preservation under a natural-language-inference check; T(c) \in [0,1] is technical-term preservation, computed as a linear-sum-assignment overlap of domain terms; Q(c) \in \{0,1\} is quantitative-content preservation, requiring numerical values, units, and rounding conventions to be unchanged or within a pre-specified tolerance; and R(c) \in \{0,1\} is scope-qualifier preservation, requiring conditional clauses, population restrictions, and moderators to be retained.

A paraphrase c' is admitted to the meaning-preserving arm only if

\operatorname{Admit}(c \to c') = \mathbf{1}[E(c,c')=1] \cdot \mathbf{1}[T(c,c') \geq \tau_T] \cdot \mathbf{1}[Q(c,c')=1] \cdot \mathbf{1}[R(c,c')=1],

with the threshold \tau_T and the per-claim-type admission rules fixed at calibration. Claims for which no paraphrase satisfies the vector are excluded from the confirmatory study, and the exclusion rate is reported by claim type. Semantic preservation thus becomes a reported characteristic of the stimulus set rather than a binary gate, making visible the fact that “meaning preservation” is a constraint set whose satisfiability varies across scientific claim types.

3.3 Claim-type stratification

Claim type enters as a stratification factor. For mechanistic claims of the form “X causes Z via Y,” T(c) must constrain the referential granularity of mediator terms, and paraphrases that shift granularity are rejected regardless of E(c). For quantitative claims of the form “X changes by N,” Q(c) is binding and numerical rewriting is prohibited in the meaning-preserving arm. For scope-qualified claims of the form “under C, X affects Y,” R(c) is binding; because the scope qualifier is retained in the meaning-preserving arm, scope qualifiers cannot simultaneously serve as ablatable provenance components within the same claim set, and the provenance-component analysis is accordingly restricted to factual and methodological claims in which no scope qualifier functions as a provenance component.

4. Design

4.1 Factorial core

Factor A: Provenance level (four nested levels). The levels are P_3 (full provenance: persistent identifier, paragraph locator, verbatim excerpt, claim–paragraph relation, scope qualifier), P_2 (identifier and verbatim excerpt), P_1 (identifier only), and P_0 (no provenance). The four-level structure is the minimum permitting component-necessity testing, and the P_1 versus P_0 contrast isolates the effect of the persistent identifier alone.

Factor B: Semantic condition. The confirmatory conditions are S_{\text{pres}} (admitted by the preservation vector) and S_{\text{free}} (language-model free summarization, with semantic drift measured and reported as a continuous covariate rather than treated as a fixed condition). A multi-hop extension within the free arm—two- and three-step summarization chains—is reserved for exploratory model comparison and is not crossed with provenance in the confirmatory study.

The confirmatory core is 4 \times 2 = 8 conditions. The primary causal contrast is

\Delta_{\text{prov}} = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid S_{\text{pres}}, P_0] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid S_{\text{pres}}, P_3],

estimated as the coefficient on provenance level within the S_{\text{pres}} stratum. This is the only contrast under which semantic content is held at the preservation vector and therefore the only contrast licensing a causal interpretation of provenance absence.

4.2 Outcome: stratified reconstruction levels

The outcome is a sequence of layers: L_0, relevant source not recovered; L_1, correct source identified but relevant paragraph not located; L_2, correct source and relevant paragraph located; L_3, L_2 plus support relation correctly judged as supports, qualifies, contradicts, or ambiguous; L_4, L_3 plus scope and limiting conditions correctly identified. The primary endpoint is L_3 or above; L_3 is chosen over L_4 because scope identification is expected to approach floor in P_0 and would induce a floor effect masking provenance effects at lower layers. The full distribution is reported as a secondary outcome.

The ordinality of the sequence is treated as a hypothesis rather than a maintained assumption. At calibration, an ordinal logit model is compared with a bivariate binary model for L_1 and L_2 separately. If the residual correlation in the bivariate model differs significantly from zero, the proportional-odds assumption is rejected, and the confirmatory analysis uses either a multinomial outcome or two separately modeled binary endpoints. The partial-support logic of the WiCE annotation scheme, which provides entailment judgments over sub-sentence units and minimal evidence subsets for each subclaim , is incorporated into L_3: the three-category support scheme is refined to four categories with an explicit “ambiguous or insufficient information” option, and partial support is a substantive judgment coded separately from recovery failure.

5. Statistical Framework

5.1 Primary model

Let Y_{ijk} denote the L_3-or-above outcome for claim i, condition j, and evaluator k. For the binary endpoint,

\operatorname{logit} P(Y_{ijk}=1) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{Prov}_j + \beta_2 S_j + \beta_3 (\text{Prov}_j \times S_j) + \boldsymbol{\gamma}^{\top} \mathbf{X}_{ijk} + u_i + v_k,

where \text{Prov}_j is coded as three orthogonal contrasts—P_1{-}P_0, P_2{-}P_1, and P_3{-}P_2—so that \beta_1 estimates the identifier effect, P_2{-}P_1 the excerpt effect, and P_3{-}P_2 the locator-and-relation effect; S_j is the semantic-condition indicator; u_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_u^2) is a claim random intercept; v_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_v^2) is an evaluator random intercept; and \mathbf{X} includes claim type, source accessibility, and measured evaluator retrieval skill.

The interaction coefficient \beta_3 tests mechanism separability. If \beta_3 \approx 0 and \beta_1 \neq 0, provenance absence and semantic transformation act additively, and provenance carries an independent effect under semantic preservation. If \beta_3 \neq 0 while \beta_1 \approx 0, provenance matters only once semantic drift has occurred—a conditional finding of substantive interest rather than a failure of the design.

5.2 Equivalence and null-result reporting

Superiority testing alone cannot distinguish “no evidence of an effect” from “evidence of no effect.” Two one-sided equivalence tests are pre-registered alongside the superiority test for RQ1. With the smallest effect size of interest \delta_{\text{SESOI}} defined as the smallest \Delta_{\text{prov}} that would be practically consequential for provenance-recording policy, the equivalence interval is [-\delta_{\text{SESOI}}, +\delta_{\text{SESOI}}]. If the 90\% confidence interval for \Delta_{\text{prov}} falls entirely within this interval, the study reports equivalence within the pre-specified margin; if the interval excludes zero but also excludes the equivalence interval, the study reports a detectable but not practically consequential effect. This tripartite reporting scheme is fixed at pre-registration and prevents post hoc construction of the null-result narrative.

5.3 Retrieval skill as a measured covariate

In the P_0 condition, the evaluator must generate search terms, select databases, and decide when to stop. Variance in retrieval skill may dominate the provenance effect. A random evaluator intercept absorbs mean differences but not skill-dependent slopes, and with 20–30 evaluators and four provenance levels a random-slope model is likely to encounter singular fits. Retrieval skill is therefore measured directly through a brief standardized search task administered before the main task, and the resulting score enters \mathbf{X}_{ijk} as a covariate. The pre-registered fallback if the skill score is unavailable for some evaluators is a sensitivity model with multiple imputation, not reliance on random slopes.

5.4 Non-inferiority as a calibration-gated endpoint

Let \widehat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}} be the calibration-stage estimate of the full-versus-none provenance effect under S_{\text{pres}}, and let M_{\text{NI}} be the non-inferiority margin for the minimal sufficient record. The anchoring requirement is M_{\text{NI}} < \Delta_{\text{prov}}, with the operational rule M_{\text{NI}} = 0.5 \widehat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}}.

The pre-registered go/no-go rule is as follows. If the lower bound of the 95\% interval for \widehat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}} exceeds 0.08 in L_3-or-above success, the confirmatory study proceeds with the non-inferiority test as a co-primary endpoint at M_{\text{NI}} = 0.5 \widehat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}}. If the estimate lies between 0.04 and 0.08, the non-inferiority test is retained as a secondary endpoint and the minimal-sufficient-record claim is reported with an explicit statement that the margin was constrained by calibration estimates. If the estimate falls below 0.04, the non-inferiority question is reclassified as exploratory: the study reports that at the observed effect size, non-inferiority cannot be established at a policy-relevant margin with the available sample. This rule makes explicit that a small or null calibration effect does not invalidate the study; it changes the question the study can answer.

5.5 Model comparison and the half-life reporting constraint

The exponential decay model is one candidate among linear-restricted, threshold, piecewise, spline, condition-specific, and non-monotonic alternatives. The half-life parameter k_{1/2} = \ln(2)/\lambda is reported only if the exponential model wins on leave-one-out information criterion against all pre-specified competitors; the 95\% interval for the decay parameter excludes zero; an asymptote is estimable with a finite upper bound; and at least six to eight non-zero exposure levels are available. The four-level provenance design yields three non-zero levels. Therefore, in the confirmatory study as designed, condition on exposure levels is not met, and model-comparison results are descriptive only. They cannot support or reject the exponential-decay hypothesis, and no half-life estimate is reported as a substantive quantity. The scientific claims of the dissertation use the phrase provenance-associated verifiability degradation.

6. The Language-Model Arm as a Diagnostic Instrument

The value of the language-model arm does not depend on its accuracy matching the human baseline. Benchmarking of language-model peer reviewers across four dimensions reveals that each system exhibits a distinct specialization profile with characteristic blind spots—failure modes that aggregate metrics miss entirely—and that no single system sustains performance across all dimensions simultaneously . The relevant question is therefore error-structural: does provenance absence push a fixed-retrieval language-model agent toward confident but incorrect reconstruction, while pushing human evaluators toward explicit abstention?

Outcomes are coded as (C) correct reconstruction, (A) explicit abstention with a report that provenance is insufficient, and (W) confident-but-wrong reconstruction. The primary language-model analysis is the conditional distribution of (C, A, W) across provenance levels. The secondary analysis is the divergence between the human and language-model conditional distributions. A finding that the ratio \Pr(W \mid P_0)/\Pr(W \mid P_3) is larger for the language model while \Pr(A \mid P_0)/\Pr(A \mid P_3) is smaller for the language model would support a calibration interpretation: provenance metadata functions to support the alignment between confidence and correctness, not merely to raise accuracy. This analysis does not require the language model and human effect sizes to agree on the L_3-or-above metric, and it therefore avoids the construct-validity problem of treating a language model as a stand-in for an independent human verifier. The large human–model gap documented by CiteME—4.2–18.5% model accuracy against 69.7% human accuracy —establishes that the two populations are not interchangeable, which is precisely why their error structures, rather than their aggregate accuracies, are the appropriate object of comparison.

7. Confirmatory Parameters

The confirmatory target is 120–200 claims crossed with the eight conditions, with 20–30 human evaluators assigned so that each claim is evaluated under all eight conditions and each evaluator sees each claim in exactly one condition. Power for the primary contrast is computed from the calibration-stage estimate of the between-claim variance \sigma_u^2 and the residual variance. Because equivalence testing requires larger samples than superiority testing for the same effect size, \delta_{\text{SESOI}} is set at the calibration-stage minimum detectable effect at 80\% power, floored at 5 percentage points; if the calibration-stage variance is so large that this floor is exceeded, the equivalence test is underpowered and the confirmatory study reports only the superiority test with an explicit limitation statement.

Condition labels are not shown to evaluators, but P_1 and P_0 are partially self-revealing. Calibration includes a speculation–behavior sub-study in which evaluators report, after each stimulus, their inferred condition level, their expected likelihood of success, and the maximum search time they are willing to invest. If inferred condition correlates with expected success or with maximum search time, the confirmatory instruction set is revised to decouple inferred condition from motivation, and residual speculation effects are reported as a sensitivity analysis.

The contrast between P_1 and P_0 is pre-registered as a secondary endpoint with its own equivalence test. If the identifier-only record is non-inferior to the full record within the pre-specified margin, the result carries direct policy implication: existing identifier infrastructure is sufficient for paragraph-level reconstruction under semantic preservation, and additional metadata requirements are not justified by reconstruction performance.

8. Theoretical Propositions and Their Operational Tests

Proposition 1: Provenance insufficiency. A persistent identifier alone does not guarantee paragraph localization or support judgment. Test. In P_1, L_1 success exceeds L_2 success by a margin at least as large as the L_2–L_3 difference. If L_1 \approx L_2, the identifier is functionally sufficient for localization within that claim stratum, and the proposition is weakened for that stratum.

Proposition 2: Mechanism separability. The effect of provenance absence under semantic preservation is statistically distinguishable from the effect of semantic transformation. Test. \beta_1 in the S_{\text{pres}} stratum is nonzero, and the interaction \beta_3 is either null, indicating additive separability, or signed such that provenance matters more under free summarization, indicating conditional separability. A null \beta_1 with a nonzero \beta_3 is interpretable as provenance mattering only after semantic drift has occurred.

Proposition 3: Conditional minimum sufficiency. There exists a provenance-component subset that is non-inferior to the full record within M_{\text{NI}}, and the identity of that subset may vary by claim type. Test. Conditional on the calibration-stage go/no-go rule. If the rule triggers the exploratory reclassification, the proposition is not tested; the study reports the calibration-stage effect size and the power constraint that prevented the test.

Proposition 4: Non-universal degradation. Degradation is not assumed to be monotonic or exponential. Condition-specific description is the default, and the exponential model is a rejected-by-default candidate unless the reporting constraints of §5.5 are satisfied.

9. Roadmap and Calibration Outputs

The protocol proceeds through five stages. Stage 0 freezes the systematic documentary audit: for each prior-art family, the audit records whether provenance is experimentally manipulated, whether semantic content is constrained at a meaning-preserving level, whether the dependent variable includes paragraph-level reconstruction, and whether human independent reconstruction is the criterion. Stage 1 is a calibration study of 10–16 claims with 3–4 evaluators, producing five outputs that jointly determine the confirmatory design: the semantic-preservation admission rate by claim type; the L_1–L_2 ordinality test result; the calibration-stage estimate \widehat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}} and variance components for power calculation and the go/no-go rule; the proportion of P_0 outcome variance explained by retrieval skill; and the correlation between inferred condition and motivation. Stage 2 freezes the confirmatory pre-registration: condition count, claim-sampling rule, primary endpoint, \delta_{\text{SESOI}}, equivalence intervals, M_{\text{NI}}, model structure, fallback rules, exclusion rules, and the P_1-versus-P_0 secondary equivalence test. Stage 3 collects confirmatory data at the scale specified in §7. Stage 4 executes the pre-registered analysis and reports equivalence results alongside superiority results. Stage 5 releases the provenance-ablation stimulus set, the preservation-vector admission log, the pre-registration protocol, and the analysis code.

The success criterion of the dissertation is the production of a validated causal measurement framework for claim–evidence traceability, not the confirmation of a preselected curve. The strongest result may be a threshold, an interaction, a null effect under equivalence, or a non-monotonic pattern. Each of these outcomes is pre-registered as a reportable scientific conclusion, and each redirects the policy question: if provenance absence under semantic preservation has no practically consequential effect, provenance-recording policy should shift from metadata mandates toward semantic-fidelity interventions; if the identifier-only record is non-inferior to the full record, existing citation infrastructure is sufficient for paragraph-level reconstruction and additional metadata requirements are over-engineered. The framework is designed so that these outcomes—including those unfavorable to the full-record narrative—are powered, interpretable, and publishable.

