Appendix A. Notation and Formal Preliminaries

Let \mathcal{C} = \{c_1, \ldots, c_N\} denote the claim population, \mathcal{E} = \{e_1, \ldots, e_K\} the evaluator population, and \mathcal{J} = \mathcal{P} \times \mathcal{S} the confirmatory condition set, with provenance levels \mathcal{P} = \{P_0, P_1, P_2, P_3\} and semantic conditions \mathcal{S} = \{S_{\text{pres}}, S_{\text{free}}\}, so that |\mathcal{J}| = 8. For claim i \in \mathcal{C}, evaluator k \in \mathcal{E}, and condition j = (p, s) \in \mathcal{J}, let Y_{ijk} \in \{0,1,2,3,4\} denote the realized reconstruction level, with the layer semantics fixed in Appendix B. Let \mathbf{X}_{ijk} \in \mathbb{R}^d denote the covariate vector whose components are specified in Appendix G. Let A_{ijk} \in \{0,1\} denote the binary L_3-or-above endpoint, A_{ijk} = \mathbf{1}[Y_{ijk} \geq 3].

The design assignment is a map \pi: \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{J} such that each claim is evaluated under each condition exactly once and each evaluator sees each claim under exactly one condition. The assignment \pi is randomized subject to the balance constraints specified in Appendix G.3; randomization is the basis for the identification argument of Appendix E.

Throughout, \mathbb{E} and \operatorname{Var} denote expectation and variance under the design distribution induced by \pi and the sampling of claims from \mathcal{C}. The symbol \perp\!\!\!\perp denotes probabilistic independence, and \mathbf{1}[\cdot] the indicator function.

