Appendix B. Construct Operators and the Layer Semantics

B.1 Construct operators

Define the following measurement operators, each mapping a claim–condition–evaluator triple to a codomain:

\begin{aligned}
I &: \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{J} \times \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{S}^{*} \cup \{\varnothing\}, && \text{(source identity)} \\
\Lambda &: \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{J} \times \mathcal{E} \to 2^{\mathcal{L}} \cup \{\varnothing\}, && \text{(locator set)} \\
\Sigma &: \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{J} \times \mathcal{E} \to \{\text{supp}, \text{qual}, \text{contr}, \text{amb}\}, && \text{(support judgment)} \\
\Theta &: \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{J} \times \mathcal{E} \to 2^{\mathcal{Q}} \cup \{\varnothing\}, && \text{(scope retention)}
\end{aligned}

where \mathcal{S}^{*} is the set of source artefacts (publications, datasets, reports), \mathcal{L} is the set of locators (sentence, paragraph, table, figure, supplement, passage set), and \mathcal{Q} is the set of scope and limiting conditions. Let \mathcal{G}(c) = (V_c, E_c) denote the gold claim–evidence graph for claim c, constructed before any transformation by independent expert annotation, with V_c containing typed nodes for the claim, source set, sufficient passage sets, essential limitations, and required supplementary material, and E_c containing typed edges for support, contradiction, partial support, derivation, and scope restriction.

B.2 Layer semantics

The reconstruction level Y_{ijk} is defined by composition of the operators against the gold graph:

Y_{ijk} = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } I(c_i, j, e_k) = \varnothing \text{ or } I(c_i,j,e_k) \notin V_c^{\text{src}} \\
1 & \text{if } I(c_i,j,e_k) \in V_c^{\text{src}} \text{ and } \Lambda(c_i,j,e_k) \cap V_c^{\text{loc}} = \varnothing \\
2 & \text{if } \Lambda(c_i,j,e_k) \cap V_c^{\text{loc}} \neq \varnothing \text{ and } \Sigma(c_i,j,e_k) \notin V_c^{\text{sup}} \\
3 & \text{if } \Sigma(c_i,j,e_k) \in V_c^{\text{sup}} \text{ and } \Theta(c_i,j,e_k) \not\supseteq V_c^{\text{scope}} \\
4 & \text{if } \Sigma(c_i,j,e_k) \in V_c^{\text{sup}} \text{ and } \Theta(c_i,j,e_k) \supseteq V_c^{\text{scope}}
\end{cases}

where V_c^{\text{src}}, V_c^{\text{loc}}, V_c^{\text{sup}}, and V_c^{\text{scope}} are the source, locator, support-label, and scope node subsets of V_c. The definition makes explicit that L_2 requires locator membership in the annotated sufficient passage set, not exact string match; a paraphrased but correctly located passage satisfies L_2 provided it lies within an annotated sufficient passage. Exact locator recovery is recorded as a secondary outcome but is not the criterion for L_2.

B.3 Partial support

For claims admitting decomposition into subclaims c_i = \bigvee_{m} c_{i,m}, following the WiCE annotation logic, the support operator returns a vector \Sigma(c_i) = (\sigma_1, \ldots, \sigma_M) with \sigma_m \in \{\text{supp}, \text{qual}, \text{contr}, \text{amb}\}. A claim is coded L_3-or-above if the evaluator’s returned vector matches the gold vector on at least the subclaims designated as essential in V_c. Essentiality is annotated during gold construction and frozen before transformation.

