Appendix D. Claim–Evidence Graph: Formalization

D.1 Graph structure

A claim–evidence graph is a tuple \mathcal{G} = (V, E, \tau_V, \tau_E, \ell) where V is a finite node set, E \subseteq V \times V a directed edge set forming a DAG, \tau_V: V \to \mathcal{T}_V a node-type map, \tau_E: E \to \mathcal{T}_E an edge-type map, and \ell: V \cup E \to \Sigma^{*} a labelling function into string labels.

Node types: \mathcal{T}_V = \{\text{claim}, \text{source}, \text{passage}, \text{limitation}, \text{supplement}, \text{transformation}, \text{agent}\}. Edge types: \mathcal{T}_E = \{\text{supported\_by}, \text{contradicted\_by}, \text{partially\_supported\_by}, \text{derived\_from}, \text{restricted\_by}, \text{transformed\_by}, \text{attributed\_to}\}.

D.2 PROV compatibility

The node and edge types are chosen so that \mathcal{G} embeds into the PROV data model: claim and passage nodes are prov:Entity, transformation nodes are prov:Activity, evaluator and annotator nodes are prov:Agent, derived_from and transformed_by are prov:Derivation and prov:WasGeneratedBy, and attributed_to is prov:WasAttributedTo. The embedding is formalized as a structure-preserving map \phi: \mathcal{G} \to \mathcal{G}_{\text{PROV}} and is verified at calibration by a schema validator.

D.3 Transformation log

Every transformation applied to a claim is recorded as a transformation node v_\tau with attributes (\text{model}, \text{version}, \text{prompt}, \text{temperature}, \text{seed}, \text{date}, \text{token\_count}_{\text{in}}, \text{token\_count}_{\text{out}}, \text{compression\_ratio}). The transformation log is the set of all such nodes, and it is released with the stimulus set. The log is what permits the confirmatory analysis to condition on transformation-generator identity and to diagnose whether any apparent provenance effect is confounded with a generator-specific behaviour.

---

Appendix E. Identification of the Causal Estimand

E.1 Estimand

The target estimand is the average causal effect of provenance absence under semantic preservation:

\Delta_{\text{prov}} = \mathbb{E}\big[Y(P_0, S_{\text{pres}}) - Y(P_3, S_{\text{pres}})\big],

where Y(p,s) denotes the potential outcome under provenance level p and semantic condition s, with the expectation over the claim and evaluator populations.

E.2 Assumptions

(A1) Consistency. Y_{ijk} = Y_{ijk}(p_j, s_j) for the assigned condition j = (p_j, s_j).

(A2) Randomization. The assignment \pi is independent of potential outcomes conditional on claim and evaluator covariates: \pi \perp\!\!\!\perp \{Y(p,s)\}_{p,s} \mid (\mathbf{X}_i, \mathbf{X}_k).

(A3) Positivity. For every claim i and evaluator k, \Pr(\pi(i,k) = j) > 0 for all j \in \mathcal{J}.

(A4) SUTVA. No interference between conditions: an evaluator’s outcome under one condition is unaffected by the conditions assigned to other evaluators or to the same evaluator on other claims.

E.3 Proposition (Identification)

Proposition E.1. Under A1–A4,

\Delta_{\text{prov}} = \mathbb{E}\big[Y \mid S_{\text{pres}}, P_0\big] - \mathbb{E}\big[Y \mid S_{\text{pres}}, P_3\big].

Proof. By A1, \mathbb{E}[Y \mid S_{\text{pres}}, P_0] = \mathbb{E}[Y(P_0, S_{\text{pres}}) \mid S_{\text{pres}}, P_0]. By A2, the condition (S_{\text{pres}}, P_0) is independent of potential outcomes conditional on covariates, so \mathbb{E}[Y(P_0, S_{\text{pres}}) \mid S_{\text{pres}}, P_0, \mathbf{X}] = \mathbb{E}[Y(P_0, S_{\text{pres}}) \mid \mathbf{X}]. Marginalizing over \mathbf{X} and applying the same argument to (S_{\text{pres}}, P_3), the difference of conditional expectations equals the difference of potential-outcome expectations, which is \Delta_{\text{prov}}. \square

E.4 Corollary (Non-identification under the confounded contrast)

The contrast \mathbb{E}[Y \mid P_0] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid P_3] marginal over semantic condition does not identify \Delta_{\text{prov}}, because the marginal contrast mixes the semantic composition of the strata. Only the stratum-specific contrast under S_{\text{pres}} identifies the provenance effect. This is the formal statement of why the factorial design is necessary: the confounded contrast is a weighted average of the provenance effect and the semantic effect, and without orthogonal manipulation the weights are not identified.

E.5 Interaction

The interaction estimand is

\Delta_{\text{int}} = \big[\mathbb{E}(Y \mid S_{\text{free}}, P_0) - \mathbb{E}(Y \mid S_{\text{free}}, P_3)\big] - \big[\mathbb{E}(Y \mid S_{\text{pres}}, P_0) - \mathbb{E}(Y \mid S_{\text{pres}}, P_3)\big],

identified under A1–A4 by the same argument applied to each stratum. \Delta_{\text{int}} = 0 corresponds to additive separability; \Delta_{\text{int}} \neq 0 corresponds to conditional separability.

---

Appendix F. Ordinality Testing and the Outcome Decision Rule

F.1 Hypothesis

Let Y_{ijk} \in \{0,1,2,3,4\} be the reconstruction level. The proportional-odds model assumes \operatorname{logit} \Pr(Y \geq \ell) = \theta_\ell - \boldsymbol{\beta}^{\top} \mathbf{Z}_{ijk} for all \ell \in \{1,2,3,4\}, with a common \boldsymbol{\beta} across thresholds. The null of ordinality is the proportional-odds restriction; the alternative is the unrestricted multinomial model.

F.2 Test

At calibration, fit (i) the ordinal logit model and (ii) the bivariate binary model with endpoints A^{(1)} = \mathbf{1}[Y \geq 1] and A^{(2)} = \mathbf{1}[Y \geq 2]. Let \hat{\rho} denote the residual correlation in (ii). The decision rule is:

\text{Outcome} = \begin{cases}
\text{ordinal} & \text{if } |\hat{\rho}| \leq \rho^{*} \text{ and } \mathrm{CI}_{95}(\hat{\rho}) \ni 0 \\
\text{bivariate binary} & \text{if } |\hat{\rho}| > \rho^{*} \text{ or } \mathrm{CI}_{95}(\hat{\rho}) \not\ni 0
\end{cases}

with \rho^{*} = 0.20 fixed at pre-registration. Under the bivariate branch, the confirmatory analysis pre-registers two separately modeled binary endpoints, L_1-or-above and L_2-or-above, with a Holm correction across the two tests. Under the ordinal branch, the primary analysis uses the ordinal mixed model with a single coefficient vector.

F.3 Proposition (Consequence of misclassification)

Proposition F.1. If the true data-generating process is bivariate binary with nonzero residual correlation but the analysis uses an ordinal model, the estimator of the provenance coefficient is biased toward zero, because the ordinal model imposes a common slope across thresholds that are generated by different slopes.

Proof. The ordinal model’s common-slope restriction is a linear constraint on the threshold-specific slopes. Under the bivariate binary truth with \rho \neq 0, the threshold-specific slopes differ. The restricted estimator is the projection of the unrestricted estimator onto the constraint set, and the projection is not equal to either threshold-specific slope when the slopes differ in sign or magnitude. Averaging over the constraint yields attenuation toward the constraint surface, which for a contrast of interest is attenuation toward zero. \square

The decision rule therefore protects against a specific, predictable bias rather than a generic model-selection concern.

---

Appendix G. Statistical Model Specification

G.1 Primary model

\operatorname{logit} P(A_{ijk} = 1) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 D^{(1)}_{j} + \beta_2 D^{(2)}_{j} + \beta_3 D^{(3)}_{j} + \beta_4 S_j + \beta_5 (D^{(1)}_j S_j) + \beta_6 (D^{(2)}_j S_j) + \beta_7 (D^{(3)}_j S_j) + \boldsymbol{\gamma}^{\top} \mathbf{X}_{ijk} + u_i + v_k

with orthogonal provenance contrasts

D^{(1)}_j = \mathbf{1}[p_j = P_1] - \mathbf{1}[p_j = P_0], \quad
D^{(2)}_j = \mathbf{1}[p_j = P_2] - \mathbf{1}[p_j = P_1], \quad
D^{(3)}_j = \mathbf{1}[p_j = P_3] - \mathbf{1}[p_j = P_2],

and S_j = \mathbf{1}[s_j = S_{\text{free}}]. The primary contrast \Delta_{\text{prov}} corresponds to \beta_1 + \beta_2 + \beta_3 evaluated at S_j = 0, and the interaction \Delta_{\text{int}} corresponds to \beta_5 + \beta_6 + \beta_7.

G.2 Covariate vector

\mathbf{X}_{ijk} contains: claim type (mechanistic, quantitative, factual, methodological), source accessibility (archived, open-access, paywalled), the measured retrieval-skill score (Appendix G.4), and evaluator expertise stratum (domain expert, trained non-expert). All continuous covariates are standardized using calibration-stage means and standard deviations.

G.3 Randomization and balance

Assignment \pi is generated by permuting the eight condition labels within each claim, subject to the constraint that each evaluator receives each condition a number of times differing by at most one across the full design, and that no evaluator receives the same claim under more than one condition. Balance is verified by comparing the distribution of \mathbf{X} across conditions using the standardized mean difference; a covariate with |\text{SMD}| > 0.10 is flagged and enters a sensitivity model with inverse-probability weighting.

G.4 Retrieval skill measurement

Before the main task, each evaluator completes two calibration queries with known targets. The skill score is the standardized sum of inverse time-to-target and inverse search-action count across the two queries:

r_k = \operatorname{scale}\!\left( \sum_{q=1}^{2} \left[ \frac{1}{T_{qk}} + \frac{1}{n_{qk}} \right] \right),

where T_{qk} is time-to-target and n_{qk} is the number of search actions. The score enters \mathbf{X}_{ijk}. The calibration-stage analysis reports the proportion of P_0 outcome variance explained by r_k; if this proportion exceeds 0.30, the confirmatory evaluator sample is increased or the design is restricted to a single expertise stratum.

G.5 Estimation

The mixed model is estimated by adaptive Gauss–Hermite quadrature with at least 11 quadrature points per random effect, or by Laplace approximation with a sensitivity check at 15 points. If the fit does not converge or the estimated random-effect variance is on the boundary, the pre-registered fallback is:

1. Refit without the evaluator random intercept v_k, retaining the claim random intercept u_i and the retrieval-skill covariate.
2. If convergence still fails, fit a generalized estimating equation with an exchangeable working correlation within claims and robust standard errors.
3. Report the fallback, its reason, and a comparison of the fallback estimate with the primary specification where the primary specification was estimable on a reduced model.

The fallback ordering is fixed at pre-registration and is not selected post hoc.

G.6 Proposition (Covariate adjustment and confounding)

Proposition G.1. Under A1–A4 and conditional independence of retrieval skill from condition assignment (guaranteed by randomization), adjustment for r_k does not introduce collider bias into \Delta_{\text{prov}}.

Proof. Randomization of \pi implies r_k \perp\!\!\!\perp \pi(i,k), so r_k is a pre-treatment covariate with respect to condition assignment. Adjustment for a pre-treatment covariate that is a cause of the outcome and not a descendant of the treatment is a standard covariate adjustment that reduces variance without inducing collider bias. \square

