Appendix H. Equivalence Testing

H.1 Hypotheses

Let \delta_{\text{SESOI}} denote the smallest effect size of interest. The equivalence hypotheses for the primary contrast are

H_0^{\text{eq}}: |\Delta_{\text{prov}}| \geq \delta_{\text{SESOI}} \quad \text{versus} \quad H_1^{\text{eq}}: |\Delta_{\text{prov}}| < \delta_{\text{SESOI}}.

H.2 TOST procedure

The two one-sided tests are

T_1 = \frac{\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}} + \delta_{\text{SESOI}}}{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}})}, \qquad
T_2 = \frac{\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}} - \delta_{\text{SESOI}}}{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}})},

with equivalence declared at level \alpha if T_1 > t_{1-\alpha} and T_2 < -t_{1-\alpha}.

H.3 Proposition (CI equivalence)

Proposition H.1. The TOST procedure at level \alpha rejects H_0^{\text{eq}} if and only if the (1 - 2\alpha) confidence interval for \Delta_{\text{prov}} lies entirely within (-\delta_{\text{SESOI}}, \delta_{\text{SESOI}}).

Proof. Standard duality of one-sided tests and confidence bounds: T_1 > t_{1-\alpha} is equivalent to \hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}} - t_{1-\alpha}\widehat{\mathrm{SE}} > -\delta_{\text{SESOI}}, and T_2 < -t_{1-\alpha} is equivalent to \hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}} + t_{1-\alpha}\widehat{\mathrm{SE}} < \delta_{\text{SESOI}}. The intersection of the two is the inclusion of the (1-2\alpha) interval in the equivalence region. \square

H.4 Tripartite reporting rule

Let \mathrm{CI}_{90}(\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}) = [L, U]. The pre-registered reporting map is

\text{Verdict} = \begin{cases}
\text{equivalent within margin} & \text{if } [L,U] \subset (-\delta_{\text{SESOI}}, \delta_{\text{SESOI}}) \\
\text{detectable, not practically consequential} & \text{if } 0 \notin [L,U] \text{ and } [L,U] \not\subset (-\delta_{\text{SESOI}}, \delta_{\text{SESOI}}) \\
\text{inconclusive} & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

The three-way partition prevents the null-result narrative from being constructed post hoc and makes the equivalence conclusion a first-class scientific output.

H.5 SESOI anchoring

The SESOI is set at the calibration-stage minimum detectable effect at 80\% power with the planned sample, floored at 0.05. Formally,

\delta_{\text{SESOI}} = \max\!\left\{0.05,\; \operatorname{MDE}_{80\%}(\hat{\sigma}_u, \hat{\sigma}_v, N, K)\right\}.

If the resulting SESOI exceeds 0.10, the equivalence test is underpowered at the planned sample, and the confirmatory study reports only the superiority test with an explicit limitation statement. The floor at 0.05 ensures that the equivalence claim is not trivially true for an effect of no practical interest.

