Appendix I. Non-Inferiority: Margin Anchoring and Power

I.1 Hypotheses

Let \Delta_{\min} denote the minimal-sufficient-record effect, defined as the effect of replacing the full record P_3 with the minimal sufficient record P_{m} under S_{\text{pres}}. The non-inferiority hypotheses are

H_0^{\text{NI}}: \Delta_{\min} \geq M_{\text{NI}} \quad \text{versus} \quad H_1^{\text{NI}}: \Delta_{\min} < M_{\text{NI}}.

I.2 Proposition (Anchoring necessity)

Proposition I.1. If M_{\text{NI}} \geq \Delta_{\text{prov}}, the non-inferiority test has trivial power to distinguish a minimal record that is genuinely sufficient from one that is merely not worse than the full record by more than the full-record effect itself.

Proof. Suppose M_{\text{NI}} = \Delta_{\text{prov}}. Then the null hypothesis is that the minimal record loses at least as much as the full record gains relative to no provenance. A minimal record that loses, say, 0.9\Delta_{\text{prov}} would be declared non-inferior, even though it retains only 10\% of the provenance benefit. The test therefore cannot distinguish sufficiency from near-complete failure, and the power to detect a genuinely sufficient record is not the relevant quantity—the test is uninformative about the quantity of interest. Hence M_{\text{NI}} < \Delta_{\text{prov}} is necessary for the test to carry information, and the operational rule M_{\text{NI}} = 0.5\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}} ensures that a declared non-inferior record retains at least half of the full-record benefit. \square

I.3 Power function

For a one-sided test of H_0^{\text{NI}} at level \alpha, the power at true effect \Delta_{\min} = \delta is

\Pi(\delta) = \Phi\!\left( \frac{M_{\text{NI}} - \delta}{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat{\Delta}_{\min})} - z_{1-\alpha} \right).

Power is decreasing in \delta and increasing in M_{\text{NI}}. Because M_{\text{NI}} is anchored to \hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}}, the power is jointly determined by the calibration-stage provenance effect and the confirmatory sample.

I.4 Go/no-go rule

Define the indicator

G = \begin{cases}
\text{GO} & \text{if } L_{95}(\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}}) > 0.08 \\
\text{CONDITIONAL} & \text{if } 0.04 \leq L_{95}(\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}}) \leq 0.08 \\
\text{NO-GO} & \text{if } L_{95}(\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}}) < 0.04
\end{cases}

with L_{95} the lower 95\% confidence bound. Under GO, M_{\text{NI}} = 0.5\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}} and the non-inferiority test is a co-primary endpoint. Under CONDITIONAL, the non-inferiority test is a secondary endpoint with an explicit margin-constraint statement. Under NO-GO, the non-inferiority question is exploratory and the study reports the calibration-stage effect and the power constraint. The rule is stated in terms of the lower confidence bound rather than the point estimate to avoid the inflation of the go decision under sampling variability.

I.5 Proposition (Monotonicity of the decision)

Proposition I.2. The go/no-go rule is monotone in the calibration effect: if the rule triggers GO at \hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}}, it also triggers GO at any larger value.

Proof. The regions are defined by intervals on L_{95}(\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}}) that are ordered and non-overlapping, with the GO region above the CONDITIONAL region above the NO-GO region. Monotonicity follows from the ordering. \square

