Appendix L. Power Analysis

L.1 Primary contrast

The primary contrast \Delta_{\text{prov}} is estimated as a linear combination of the provenance coefficients in the S_{\text{pres}} stratum. Its variance under the design is approximated by

\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}) \approx \frac{\sigma^2_{\text{res}}}{N K} \left[ \frac{1}{p_{P_0}} + \frac{1}{p_{P_3}} \right] + \sigma_u^2 \left[ \frac{1}{N} \right] + \sigma_v^2 \left[ \frac{1}{K} \right],

where p_{P_0} and p_{P_3} are the marginal probabilities of assignment to P_0 and P_3 within the stratum, and \sigma^2_{\text{res}} is the residual variance on the latent scale. The formula reflects the three variance components of the mixed model: claim-level, evaluator-level, and residual.

L.2 Power for the superiority test

\Pi_{\text{sup}} = \Phi\!\left( \frac{|\Delta_{\text{prov}}|}{\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}})}} - z_{1-\alpha/2} \right).

The sample-size requirement at power 1-\beta is obtained by solving \Pi_{\text{sup}} = 1-\beta for N and K. The calibration-stage estimates \hat{\sigma}_u^2, \hat{\sigma}_v^2, and \hat{\sigma}^2_{\text{res}} are substituted.

L.3 Power for the equivalence test

Because the equivalence test requires the 90\% interval to lie within the margin, the power for the equivalence test at true effect \delta is

\Pi_{\text{eq}}(\delta) = \Phi\!\left( \frac{\delta_{\text{SESOI}} - \delta}{\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}})}} - z_{1-\alpha} \right) - \Phi\!\left( \frac{-\delta_{\text{SESOI}} - \delta}{\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}})}} + z_{1-\alpha} \right),

which is maximized at \delta = 0 and decreases as |\delta| approaches \delta_{\text{SESOI}}. Equivalence testing requires larger samples than superiority testing for the same effect size, and the SESOI floor of 0.05 is set to keep the required sample within the feasibility envelope of 20–30 evaluators and 120–200 claims.

L.4 Simulation-based power

The closed-form approximations in L.1–L.3 are supplemented by a simulation-based power analysis at calibration. The simulation draws \sigma_u, \sigma_v, and residual variance from their calibration-stage posterior approximations, generates synthetic outcomes under the fitted model, refits the model, and records the rejection frequencies for the superiority and equivalence tests across a grid of effect sizes. The simulation-based power is reported alongside the closed-form approximation; the two are required to agree within 0.05 for the design to be declared adequately powered. If they do not agree, the simulation-based estimate governs the sample-size decision.

