Appendix M. Failure Taxonomy: Formal Specification

M.1 Typed failure set

Let \mathcal{F} = \{F_1, \ldots, F_{10}\} with:

· F_1: wrong source;
· F_2: correct source, wrong locator;
· F_3: correct locator, inadequate evidence;
· F_4: over-generalization;
· F_5: omitted limitation;
· F_6: missed contradiction;
· F_7: retrieval or access failure;
· F_8: semantic transformation error;
· F_9: evaluator uncertainty;
· F_{10}: genuinely indeterminate evidence.

M.2 Multi-label coding

Each outcome is assigned a subset \mathcal{F}_{ijk} \subseteq \mathcal{F}, with no constraint on cardinality. The primary analysis of failure types is the conditional distribution of \mathcal{F}_{ijk} given condition and given non-success, computed as the empirical frequency of each label and each pair of labels. The multi-label specification is required because failures interact: a correct-source-wrong-locator failure may coexist with an omitted-limitation failure when the claim is scope-qualified, and forcing a single primary cause would discard the interaction.

M.3 Proposition (Information loss from single-label coding)

Proposition M.1. For any outcome with |\mathcal{F}_{ijk}| \geq 2, single-label coding induces a loss of information about the joint distribution of failure types, and the loss is not recoverable from the marginals.

Proof. The joint distribution \Pr(F_a, F_b) is not determined by the marginals \Pr(F_a) and \Pr(F_b) unless the labels are independent, and the labels are not independent by construction (a wrong-locator failure on a scope-qualified claim is more likely to co-occur with an omitted-limitation failure than a random pair). Hence the joint information is not recoverable from single-label marginals. \square

---

Appendix N. Calibration Outputs and Decision Rules

N.1 Calibration outputs

The calibration stage produces five outputs:

1. \hat{\alpha}_s: semantic-preservation admission rate by claim type s.
2. \hat{\rho}: residual correlation from the L_1–L_2 bivariate binary model.
3. (\hat{\Delta}_{\text{prov}}^{\text{cal}}, \hat{\sigma}_u^2, \hat{\sigma}_v^2, \hat{\sigma}^2_{\text{res}}): effect and variance components.
4. \hat{\pi}_r: proportion of P_0 outcome variance explained by retrieval skill.
5. \hat{\kappa}: correlation between inferred condition and expected success or maximum search time.

N.2 Decision rules

· Claim-type inclusion: type s is included in the confirmatory set if \hat{\alpha}_s \geq 0.60. Otherwise excluded with reported exclusion effect.
· Outcome model: ordinal if |\hat{\rho}| \leq 0.20 and \mathrm{CI}_{95}(\hat{\rho}) \ni 0; bivariate binary otherwise.
· Non-inferiority: GO / CONDITIONAL / NO-GO per Appendix I.4.
· Retrieval skill: if \hat{\pi}_r > 0.30, increase evaluator N or restrict to a single expertise stratum.
· Demand characteristics: if |\hat{\kappa}| > 0.30, revise the instruction set to decouple inferred condition from motivation before confirmation.

N.3 Proposition (Joint feasibility)

Proposition N.1. The confirmatory design is feasible only if the conjunction of the five decision rules yields a non-empty confirmatory condition set.

Proof. Each rule restricts the confirmatory condition set: the claim-type rule restricts \mathcal{C}, the outcome-model rule restricts the analysis family, the non-inferiority rule restricts the endpoint set, the retrieval-skill rule restricts \mathcal{E}, and the demand-characteristics rule restricts the instruction set. If any restriction is empty (e.g., no claim type attains \hat{\alpha}_s \geq 0.60), the conjunction is empty and the confirmatory study cannot proceed as specified. The protocol therefore requires the calibration outputs to be jointly feasible, and the pre-registration is conditional on this feasibility. \square

